Whitten and Tygar first defined what "usable" must mean for security software. It is usable if the people expected to use it:

1. are reliably made aware of the security tasks they need to perform;
2. are able to figure out how to successfully perform those tasks;
3. don't make dangerous errors; and
4. are sufficiently comfortable with the interface to continue using it.

They then named five properties that make security harder to design for than other software. The **unmotivated user property**: "Security is usually a secondary goal", so people will not read manuals or hunt for hidden controls. The **abstraction property**: policies are abstract rules alien to most people. The **lack of feedback property**: it is hard to show whether a configuration does what the user "really wants". The **barn door property**: once a secret has leaked, "there is no way to be sure that it has not already been read". The **weakest link property**: one error is enough.

**"Alice in Warningland" (2013).** Devdatta Akhawe (UC Berkeley) and Adrienne Porter Felt (Google) used the telemetry of Firefox and Chrome (opt-in usage data that browsers report to their makers) to observe 25,405,944 real warning impressions in May and June 2013. Users continued through:

- malware warnings: 7.2% in Firefox, 23.2% in Chrome;
- phishing warnings: 9.1% in Firefox, 18.0% in Chrome;
- SSL warnings: 33.0% in Firefox, but 70.2% in Chrome.

So the popular belief that "users ignore all warnings" was wrong: most people heeded malware and phishing warnings. But the same kind of risk, presented differently, produced very different behaviour, which meant the design was doing much of the work.

